{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019wBazotkfBMaBTHx77bwnJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# Domain Analysis — KCNA\n11:## Exam facts (established from sources)\n27:[Omitted long matching line]\n31:## Domain inventory\n33:Objective numbering below (D1.1, D1.2, …) is a **Lodestar convention**. CNCF publishes named competencies under each domain but does **not** number them and does **not** publish sub-competency weights. The `Weight %` column is stated only at domain level because that is the only level the sources state.\n37:[Omitted long matching line]\n38:[Omitted long matching line]\n42:**Domain-weight arithmetic check:** 44 + 28 + 16 + 12 = 100. All four weights are explicitly stated in three independent cached sources (CNCF curriculum PDF, LF exam page, LF program-changes page) and agree.\n48:## Concept map\n52:### D1.1 — Kubernetes Core Concepts\n102:| Deployment eras | Traditional (physical servers) → Virtualized (VMs) → Container (shared OS, lightweight, portable). |\n106:### D1.2 — Administration\n128:### D1.3 — Scheduling\n146:### D1.4 — Containerization\n153:| Container vs VM | Containers relax isolation to share the host OS, making them lightweight; a VM includes a full guest OS. |\n164:### D2.1 — Networking\n190:### D2.2 — Security\n214:### D2.3 — Troubleshooting\n232:### D2.4 — Storage\n257:### D3.1 — Application Delivery\n298:### D3.2 — Debugging\n302:### D4.1 — Observability\n332:### D4.2 — Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\n372:### D4.3 — Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n396:## Dependency graph\n400:### Tier 0 — Root concepts (no prerequisites)\n404:### Tier 1 — Depends only on Tier 0\n418:### Tier 2 — Depends on Tier 1\n431:### Tier 3 — Depends on Tier 2\n447:### Tier 4 — Depends on Tier 3\n462:### Tier 5 — Depends on Tier 4\n476:### Tier 6 — Ecosystem and institutional (low technical prerequisite, high name-density)\n482:### Sequencing implications\n496:## Known traps\n500:### D1 — Kubernetes Fundamentals\n539:### D2 — Container Orchestration\n582:### D3 — Cloud Native Application Delivery\n602:### D4 — Cloud Native Architecture\n636:## Gaps in sources\n640:### Blocking — high confidence these are examinable, and cached coverage is absent or index-level only\n651:| G8 | **Deployment update mechanics.** Rolling update strategy, `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`, `Recreate`, rollout status, revision history, rollback. D3 doubled in weight and this is its most operationally central topic; the cached Argo CD source mentions blue/green and canary only in passing. | D3.1, D1.1 | kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/ |\n662:### Secondary — likely examinable, cached coverage thin, lower risk\n682:### Structural gaps — cannot be closed by research\n686:| G33 | **Sub-competency weights are unpublished.** CNCF states domain weights only. There is no authoritative basis for allocating the 44% of D1 across Core Concepts / Administration / Scheduling / Containerization. | Chapter-weight allocation within a domain must be authored judgment, documented as such in the outline. Do not present a derived split as if CNCF published it. |\n694:## Recommendations for the next stage\n697:2. **Fetch the LFS250 syllabus (G37) first.** It is the highest-leverage single artifact available — it is the closest thing to a detailed objective list CNCF/LF produces, and it would materially firm up the sub-competency weighting judgment that G33 otherwise leaves to guesswork.\n698:3. **Front-matter must carry three disclosures**: the 2025-11-24 blueprint change and what it invalidates in older prep material; that question count and passing score are unpublished; and that sub-competency weights are unpublished, so chapter weighting is authored judgment. All three are Ethical Guardrail obligations, not optional courtesies.\n699:4. **Build the D3 chapter around the GitOps-as-control-loop synthesis.** The four OpenGitOps principles restate the Kubernetes reconciliation pattern applied to Git. A reader who has met controllers in D1 will experience this as a genuine ☀️ Zenith rather than a fifth list to memorize — and D3 doubling in weight makes it worth the investment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28092808-bc4d-40c9-9bbe-7b3caf22edd5","uuid":"e61cd0ba-9d47-4991-bac1-76011df87a57","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:09:01.070Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"1:# Domain Analysis — KCNA\n11:## Exam facts (established from sources)\n27:[Omitted long matching line]\n31:## Domain inventory\n33:Objective numbering below (D1.1, D1.2, …) is a **Lodestar convention**. CNCF publishes named competencies under each domain but does **not** number them and does **not** publish sub-competency weights. The `Weight %` column is stated only at domain level because that is the only level the sources state.\n37:[Omitted long matching line]\n38:[Omitted long matching line]\n42:**Domain-weight arithmetic check:** 44 + 28 + 16 + 12 = 100. All four weights are explicitly stated in three independent cached sources (CNCF curriculum PDF, LF exam page, LF program-changes page) and agree.\n48:## Concept map\n52:### D1.1 — Kubernetes Core Concepts\n102:| Deployment eras | Traditional (physical servers) → Virtualized (VMs) → Container (shared OS, lightweight, portable). |\n106:### D1.2 — Administration\n128:### D1.3 — Scheduling\n146:### D1.4 — Containerization\n153:| Container vs VM | Containers relax isolation to share the host OS, making them lightweight; a VM includes a full guest OS. |\n164:### D2.1 — Networking\n190:### D2.2 — Security\n214:### D2.3 — Troubleshooting\n232:### D2.4 — Storage\n257:### D3.1 — Application Delivery\n298:### D3.2 — Debugging\n302:### D4.1 — Observability\n332:### D4.2 — Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\n372:### D4.3 — Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n396:## Dependency graph\n400:### Tier 0 — Root concepts (no prerequisites)\n404:### Tier 1 — Depends only on Tier 0\n418:###